aising"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point firmly to Sykes: a modestly Democratic-leaning district, incumbency, and a roughly 25-to-1 fundraising edge over Carey Coleman, whose campaign has also drawn negative headlines over a missing disclosure. Expert raters and prediction markets alike treat this as safely Democratic, so the forecast stays near the fundamentals-based margin with only a slight trim for the absence of general-election polling.", "watch": "A late surge of outside spending against Sykes or a strong Republican debate performance could narrow the margin, though no polling currently signals such a shift.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Emilia Sykes (D) is running."]}, "overview": "First-term incumbent Emilia Sykes seeks reelection in a northeastern Ohio district that leans slightly Democratic. She faces Republican Carey Coleman, who won a contested primary but has raised little money and drawn scrutiny over a missing campaign disclosure, along with criticism of past remarks about teachers' unions. An independent candidate is also on the ballot.\n\nSykes has built a dominant financial position, with nearly $1.9 million on hand, and has spent the year on constituent-focused events. Expert raters across the spectrum place the race in the Safe or Likely Democratic column, and prediction markets give her roughly a 95% chance of winning. With no public general-election polling, the forecast rests on fundamentals, ratings, and markets, all of which point the same direction.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9765, "margin": 13.33}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9641, "margin": 12.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.9651, "margin": 12.5}]}